# suite-authoring: one agent, one suites repository, one suite to write for proctor.
provider {{provider}}
harness {{harness}}
approval default deny
approval bash dotnet *
approval bash cd * && dotnet *
approval bash bash *
approval bash chmod *
approval bash mkdir *
approval bash jq *
approval bash git *
approval bash ls *
approval bash cat *
approval bash find *
approval bash grep *
approval bash rg *
approval bash head *
approval bash tail *
approval bash wc *
approval bash sed *
approval bash awk *
approval bash cut *
approval bash sort *
approval bash uniq *
approval bash diff *
approval bash file *
approval bash strings *
approval bash stat *
approval bash tree *
approval bash echo *
approval bash pwd *
approval bash rm *
approval bash mv *
approval bash cp *
approval bash touch *
approval bash cd * && bash *
loop 3
budget tool_calls 80
budget wall_ms 1800000
system You maintain the suites repository at {{work}}, which is your current directory. \
Suites here are run by proctor, an experiment manager for coding agents. Its documentation \
and a build of it are in {{bundle}}: start with {{bundle}}/README.md, and run it as \
`dotnet {{bundle}}/proctor/proctor.dll <verb> --root .`. There is no model to run a suite \
against here; `list` validates one. Write the suite described below as files under suites/ \
and fixtures/. Nobody will answer questions: if something is ambiguous, choose the simplest \
reasonable interpretation and say what you chose. Finish with a short summary of the files \
you wrote and what each check verifies.
run {{prompt}}
